/span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">≈</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbf">12.2</span><span class="mspace">&nbsp;</span><span class="mord mathbf">MHz</span></span></span></span></span></span> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <span><span class="katex"><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;">V</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3283em;"><span class="" style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.2222em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.0278em;">D</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span class=""></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">:</span><span class="mspace">&nbsp;</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">→</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbf">0.54</span><span class="mspace">&nbsp;</span><span class="mord mathbf" style="margin-right: 0.016em;">V</span></span></span></span></span></span>
</div>
<ul>
<li>The frequency of the simulation is <strong>12.20 MHz</strong>. An independent integration of the two equations of the circuit with SciPy (Radau method) gives <strong>12.19 MHz</strong></li>
<li>The small-signal formula gives 15.17 MHz. The oscillation is large, so the formula is only a first estimate of the real frequency</li>
<li>The diode voltage goes from <strong>0.000 V to 0.541 V</strong> in steady state, the same in the SciPy reference (the difference of the swing is 0.01 %)</li>
<li>The voltage on the diode is not a sine wave: its swing (0 to 0.54 V) is larger than the negative-resistance region (0.10 V to 0.43 V), so the waveform is distorted</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reading the Plot</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>D</strong>: the voltage on the diode and on C. The 1 ns step of the supply is a strong kick: the first swing already reaches 0.5 V after 45 ns, and the same oscillation continues up to 2 µs</li>
<li><strong>X</strong>: the voltage between Rl and L. In steady state it stays between 0.218 V and 0.256 V (a ripple of about 38 mV), because the coil blocks the high-frequency current. The current of the coil is <em>(V<sub>b</sub> − X) / R<sub>l</sub></em>, between about 0 and 6.5 mA</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to Run It</h2>
<div class="ch-card">
<p style="margin: 0;">Press <strong>Start Analysis</strong> in the analysis block (Time Domain, 0 to 2 µs, step 0.2 ns). The options <em>uic</em> (start from zero energy in L and C), <em>adaptive</em> and <em>order 2</em> are already set in the block.</p>
</div>

<h2>Things to Try</h2>
<div class="ch-card">
<ol>
<li>Set the supply <em>V1</em> of Vb to 0.05 V or to 0.6 V: the bias point is outside the negative-resistance region and the voltage on D settles to a constant (0.034 V and 0.56 V): no oscillation</li>
<li>Increase Rl to 50 Ω or to 200 Ω: the drop in Rl moves the bias point out of the negative-resistance region (D settles at 0.048 V and 0.010 V) and the oscillation does not start</li>
<li>Increase C to 400 pF or L to 4 µH: the frequency goes down (the period is proportional to √(L C)); the amplitude stays about the same, 0.52 V and 0.54 V</li>
<li>Draw the limit cycle: add <em>L.I</em> (the current of the coil) to the outputs and set <em>D</em> as the x axis. The steady state is a closed curve in the (V, I) plane</li>
</ol>
</div>
